hi everyone this is bruno aziza and  today i am talking to  cody now cody's going to talk to us  about real time and anomaly detection  one of the design patterns that we are  delivering to you this week if you  don't remember what design patterns are  in our conversation with robert last  week  on product recommendation these design  patterns are a starting point they're  there to help you  deploy a solution for a given business  problem so  anomaly detection is a big one and cody  has a ton of experience in that  i've known him for a long time he was at  dumbo for seven years and now has been  here at google  for a year so could he take us to the  genesis why does  real time matter to our customers real  time i think it matters to every company  especially this digitally transforming  age  like everything's changing i think 2020  has shown us  like very clearly that companies that  can adapt quickly  to understand customers and to  understand  competitors and the market in general  are those that ultimately win  and we found that real time really is  the solution to help them find answers  quickly so we talked about real time now  how does this real-time problem relate  to  anomaly detection the real-time input  itself is super exciting  like everybody that's like yeah of  course i want real time like  answers fast but the reality is if we  just do real time  if we just take a real time stream and  point it at a company  at a problem it could make things worse  unless there's some  more intelligence built into it so we  found that the sweet spot tends to be  combining real-time data streams with  advanced analytics  so ai ml in this case anomaly detection  so helping organizations find in the  moment  those outliers like the unique things  that they should really care about  and those could be good or bad like it  could be like this is a risk  like worry about this or like hey  something good's happening  this is different do more of this yeah  so we found to be like really sweet  with real time that's why protection is  critical we have to tell people that  these design patterns originate from  customers and partners that tell us  about their need and we go out  use the best practices we have and build  this guidance sample code  architecture guidance so you can use  those best practices at your company so  where did this  story on anomaly detection start on your  end so we had a large teleco provider  approach us and say hey i have this this  problem and originally was actually more  of a processing problem  like we have a lot of data we have this  massive network  we want to know what's like different in  our traffic  but we can't even process the data so we  started there and the awesome thing is  with tools like data flow bigquery we  were able like to meet their scaling  needs  so the requirements were 250 000 events  per second that had to be processed  they kind of kicked off this journey  like we we took that pattern we solved  the problem and we've been kind of like  wrapping it up in a pattern saying okay  does anyone else have this need for  anomaly detection  we've been amazed at how many companies  like yeah  i have a problem i could use some help  it is real time in nature  it really has turned into a kind of a  rinse and repeat that's excellent and  that's that's the whole point for why  we're building these design patterns so  we want to invite you  uh to take advantage of them right now  by clicking on the link down below and  of course  suggesting more design patterns uh that  you might need for your organization but  before we go i want to talk about  partners and how they added to the  ecosystem here and one in particular i  think confluence  that you've been working with tell us  more about that at google cloud we love  love love partners in this case  confluent has been a pretty key partner  helping us  bridge the divide between on-premise or  other clouds to gcp  it's like a lot of times people like  yeah i have this problem i'd love for  google help me solve it  but the process for doing that  integration sometimes isn't super clear  a lot of companies have invested in  kafka confluent is  basically a managed version of kafka and  so they made it very very easy for us to  create that integration and capitalize  on this functionality i will say as well  when it comes to the actual  anomaly detection we found that a number  of other companies have a lot of great  proprietary models that can be plugged  into this so our architecture is  actually somewhat transferable  the pipeline is pretty established like  we know how to do that really really  well  we know how to integrate models i'm kind  of more generic models  but we have partners that really can  augment that and bring  specialty into the actual need so  for some organizations the patterns are  like like the exact solution for others  the architecture's a solution and a  pattern and a model fits really well  into that architecture so there's value  for customers there's value prospects  listening to us  there's value for partners there's value  for everyone again  if you are interested in this design  pattern make sure you click on the link  down below if you are interested in  others that we might not have built yet  don't be shy and suggest which ones  you'd like us to build for you  cody thank you so much for the time  today i hope people can reach out to you  we'll talk to you soon
 

